  "id": "lateral-lunge",
  "name": "Lateral Lunge",
  "definition": "A side-step lunge that starts and ends in bilateral standing. The bodyweight fixture uses a lateral step equal to 60 percent of body height; the suspension fixture faces the anchor, holds both handles for balance, and uses a self-selected controlled step and depth. Hip abduction and adduction and the corresponding gluteus-medius and adductor-longus/brevis roles are transparent anatomy-and-mechanics inferences from the lateral displacement and Arnold capability map; Riemann et al. directly measured sagittal lower-limb mechanics but did not measure frontal-plane joint moments, muscle activity, or a relative muscle ranking. Adductor magnus, gracilis, and pectineus receive secondary credit specifically for the hip-adduction return through Arnold anatomy and Lovell resisted-adduction evidence; this transfer is not direct lateral-lunge EMG and does not grant magnus hip-extension credit. Forward, reverse, walking, Cossack, split-squat, lateral step-up, jumping, loaded, and other stride variants remain outside this family. TFL receives secondary credit specifically for the hip-abduction lateral-step phase, not the hip-extension return. Bouillon recorded TFL during a side-step lunge from shoulder-width standing with a leg-length-based stride; transfer to these fixtures is an explicit anatomy-and-mechanics inference through the unchanged abduction capability, not exact-protocol EMG, force, ranking or adaptation.",
  "evidenceRefs": ["arnold-2010-lower-limb", "riemann-2013-forward-lateral-lunge", "lovell-2012-hip-adductor-tests", "bouillon-2012-unilateral-weight-bearing-emg", "trx-2026-suspension-exercise-guide"],
